# Project Report: Authenticated Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange

## 1. How the Algorithms Work

### Square-and-Multiply Exponentiation
Trying to calculate large powers like $5^{15} \pmod{23}$ directly will crash or freeze a program because the raw numbers get way too big for memory. To fix this, I implemented binary square-and-multiply. 

It looks at the binary bits of the exponent from right to left. On every loop iteration, it squares the base modulo $p$. If the current bit is 1 (odd), it also multiplies the running result by the base modulo $p$. This cuts the exponent in half each step, running in $O(\log e)$ time instead of multiplying linearly. I also added input checks so it rejects negative exponents, non-positive moduli, floats, and booleans.

### Prime Generation and Checks
For prime generation, I wrapped `Crypto.Util.number.getPrime` and fed it randomness from `secrets.token_bytes`. The function validates that the generated prime has the exact bit length requested, is odd, and passes `isPrime()`. 

Whenever Bob receives a modulus $p$, he does not trust it blindly. He verifies that the bit length is strictly within the allowed 16 to 32-bit classroom range and calls `isPrime(p)` to make sure no composite numbers get through.

### Factoring and Finding a Generator
A generator $g$ has to produce every number in the multiplicative group modulo $p$, which has an order of $p - 1$. If $g$ generates a smaller subgroup, it is not a primitive root.

To test this, my code factors $p - 1$ using trial division up to $\sqrt{p - 1}$ and keeps distinct prime factors. Then, it checks candidate numbers $g \in [2, p - 2]$. For each prime factor $r$ of $p - 1$, it checks if:
$$g^{(p-1)/r} \pmod p == 1$$
If the result is 1 for any factor, $g$ is rejected. The first candidate that never produces 1 across all factors is selected.

